@anon.bcaf8a6642ef434db28a340da408f3d7.1735 = private unnamed_addr constant [940 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `warnings.warn` calls without an explicit `stacklevel` keyword\0Aargument.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe `warnings.warn` method uses a `stacklevel` of 1 by default, which\0Awill output a stack frame of the line on which the \22warn\22 method\0Ais called. Setting it to a higher number will output a stack frame\0Afrom higher up the stack.\0A\0AIt's recommended to use a `stacklevel` of 2 or higher, to give the caller\0Amore context about the warning.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Awarnings.warn(\22This is a warning\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Awarnings.warn(\22This is a warning\22, stacklevel=2)\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe because it changes\0Athe behavior of the code. Moreover, the fix will assign\0Aa stacklevel of 2, while the user may wish to assign a\0Ahigher stacklevel to address the diagnostic.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `warnings.warn`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/warnings.html#warnings.warn)\0A", align 1
