@anon.bcaf8a6642ef434db28a340da408f3d7.1755 = private unnamed_addr constant [30 x i8] c"f-string-in-get-text-func-call", align 1
@anon.bcaf8a6642ef434db28a340da408f3d7.1756 = private unnamed_addr constant [959 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for f-strings in `gettext` function calls.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIn the `gettext` API, the `gettext` function (often aliased to `_`) returns\0Aa translation of its input argument by looking it up in a translation\0Acatalog.\0A\0ACalling `gettext` with an f-string as its argument can cause unexpected\0Abehavior. Since the f-string is resolved before the function call, the\0Atranslation catalog will look up the formatted string, rather than the\0Af-string template.\0A\0AInstead, format the value returned by the function call, rather than\0Aits argument.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom gettext import gettext as _\0A\0Aname = \22Maria\22\0A_(f\22Hello, {name}!\22)  # Looks for \22Hello, Maria!\22.\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom gettext import gettext as _\0A\0Aname = \22Maria\22\0A_(\22Hello, %s!\22) % name  # Looks for \22Hello, %s!\22.\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `gettext` \E2\80\94 Multilingual internationalization services](https://docs.python.org/3/library/gettext.html)\0A", align 1
